#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tamontage — render a scripted desktop montage to video.

The montage is a JSON script (see promo/*.json). It describes a *stage* — an
unbounded virtual desktop laid out as a grid of equal window cells — a set of
windows placed on cells, a keyframed camera flying over that stage, and timed
captions. The renderer composites one output frame at a time and pipes raw
RGB24 into ffmpeg.

Two source backends fill a window, chosen per render, not per script:

    wire    procedurally drawn white-box placeholders. No game, no capture,
            renders a 50 s montage in about a minute. This is the backend you
            iterate the *timing* on.
    clip    real footage, one video file per clip id, captured from a running
            instance with `ffmpeg -window_id` (see the ta-capture skill).

Because the backend is a render-time choice, the same script that white-boxes today
renders the real thing the moment the clips exist, and re-renders when a new
version changes what the game looks like.

Stage coordinates are "window units": cell (col, row) has its top-left at
(col * pitch_x, row * pitch_y) where pitch = tile size + desktop gap. Cells may
be negative; the hero windows sit at (0,0)..(1,1) and the filler grid spreads
around them.

The camera's zoom is expressed the way you would say it out loud: `cols` is how
many window columns fit across the output frame. `cols: 1` is one window filling
the width; `cols: 40` is the wide shot. Zoom is interpolated in log space so a
pull-back reads at a constant rate rather than rushing at the end.

Usage:
    tools/tamontage render promo/tacli-promo.json -o /tmp/out.mp4
    tools/tamontage render promo/tacli-promo.json -o /tmp/out.mp4 --backend clip --clips <dir>
    tools/tamontage probe  promo/tacli-promo.json
"""

